## Instructions to write

Write task-specific `instructions` arrays on each stage, check, and unit that needs guidance. They are passed verbatim to the corresponding worker or judge and included in the plan approval question. Patterns cannot contain task-specific instructions. They supplement fixed criteria and never narrow the scope to check.

## Current material

Use the current and remaining plans, human revision instructions, and plan findings supplied above. Keep these separate from adopted outputs and optional history.

## History reference

Use fixed history references in the supplied material when needed; do not inline the history transcript into a plan.

## Confirmation guidance

Put undecidable requirements in `open_questions` for the human. Answers remain free-form and return as plan revision instructions. Do not invent a recommendation without supporting material.

## Plan contents

Use the original user request, task, repository requirements, and available patterns to propose the smallest sufficient loop. The prioritized pattern is a reference, not a constraint. You may reference any listed pattern; read its exact content at the fixed path and copy its name and the supplied content hash into `pattern`. Do not compute hashes yourself.
List undecidable requirements in `open_questions`; incorporate human clarification in the next proposal. Human-approved completion conditions define completion. Give every completion condition an appropriate verification check or human gate; the plan judge and human assess coverage.

Assign each stage a unique instance `id` and a catalog `definition` (omitting definition uses id). Reuse definitions freely with distinct instance IDs. Reuse an ID that ran in this work only when rerunning that work. A back-triggered plan must contain its return instance. Back targets are current instances or instances that ran in this work. All planned instances must be reachable from start_stage by done transitions. Ask and scalar fix target the same instance. Done may name the next instance, replan, or null.

Use only listed definitions, configured roles, configured check names, and selections resolving to documents at the fixed reference. Each plan check contains aspect, required status, target, completion_condition_ids, and optional instructions. Runner, criteria, schemas, history, and brief_by_planner come from the check registry and cannot be set by the plan. Follow loop.stage and loop.path rules and limit ceilings. Every implement instance must have its required verification later in the same segment before replan or end when the configured follows rule requires it.

Choose stage reports, unit reports, human prompts, inputs, output selections, history, gates, limits, correction roles, and instructions as needed within the rules. Output selections may name an instance or all instances of a definition. Split units have unique plan-wide IDs, descriptions, and nonoverlapping scopes. Units have no after dependencies; put dependent work in later stages. Checks, gates, questions, and replanning stay at stage level.

Pattern differences are displayed for the human and do not determine default approval. The first plan, changed completion conditions, external operations, and open questions require approval under the default policy. Under deviation, structure is compared with the last approved remaining plan. Fill external operation hashes only from verified supplied inputs. For revised proposals, incorporate human instructions and findings; for replanning, rebuild the supplied remaining work.

